Reel Edit 0.2 (test 6)
======================

Reel Edit is a video editor for RISC OS that is being written, on the MLT
multimedia framework (the engine of Shotcut and Kdenlive), with the
Raspberry Pi's video hardware through riscos-reelhwaccel. This test build
has the Viewer and a first Timeline: one video track (V1), with cutting,
trimming, deleting, undo and saving. Dissolves, dragging clips, more
tracks, titles and export come next.

It needs RISC OS 5 on ARMv7 with VFP (Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4), with
SharedUnixLibrary and ARMEABISupport (!Run checks). H.264 is decoded on
the Pi's VideoCore (1080p needs gpu_mem=128 in config.txt) and HEVC on the
Pi 4's HEVC block, the ARM taking over for anything they refuse. Sound
plays through SharedSoundBuffer, and the pictures keep time with it.

The editor
----------
Click the icon on the icon bar: the editor opens on a new project, the
Viewer (the picture, the transport and a toolbar) with the Timeline under
it.

Adding clips: drag video files from a directory display to the Timeline
(they go in where they're dropped: before the clip there, or after it if
dropped nearer its end), or to the Viewer or the icon bar icon (at the
end). An MLT project (from Reel Edit, Shotcut, Kdenlive or melt) dropped
is opened.

Files that don't say how long they are (raw MPEG-2 video, .m2v, and raw
H.264) are read through first to find their length: "Measuring" and how
far it's got show beside the transport, the desktop going on meanwhile,
and the clip goes in when it's done (files dropped meanwhile follow it).
Escape stops it. An .m2v file holds only the pictures: its sound, if it
has any, is in a file of its own (.mp2, .ac3, .wav), which will go on an
audio track when Reel Edit has them.

The Timeline: click the ruler to move the playhead (the Viewer shows that
frame), or drag along it, or drag the playhead itself, to scrub; its
timecode is shown beside it. Click a clip to select it (Adjust: select or
not); click an empty part to clear the selection. With snapping on (N, or
the Snap button), the playhead snaps to the cuts near it. The scroll
wheel scrolls the Timeline; Ctrl and the wheel zoom it, about the
pointer. The Timeline is attached under the Viewer: move or resize either
and the other follows (View > Attach the Timeline turns that off, and on
again).

The toolbar: Split (at the playhead), Delete (the selected clip; the
clips after it close up; Adjust: leave a gap), Undo, Redo, Snap; then
"Timeline zoom" - + Fit (these zoom the Timeline, never the picture), and
Save. Interactive help (!Help) says what each does.

The menu (Menu over the Viewer or the Timeline):
  File  New, Save (F3), Save as...
  Edit  Undo, Redo, Split, Trim the start, Trim the end, Delete,
        Leave gap, Clear selection
  Play  Play/Stop, Start, End
  View  Zoom the Timeline in, out, the whole project; Attach the
        Timeline; Snapping
  Video information...  the selected clip (or the one at the playhead):
        its file, length, codecs, size, frame rate, sound, and whether
        the VideoCore is decoding it; and the project's size and length

Keys (with the Viewer or the Timeline in front, clicked on):
  Space        play / stop
  J  K  L      play backwards, stop, play forwards; J or L again: faster (x2, x4)
  Left Right   a frame back, forwards (Shift: a second)
  Up Down      the cut before, after
  Home  Copy   the start, the end
  S            split at the playhead
  Q  W         trim the clip at the playhead: its start to it, its end to
               it (the clips after close up)
  Delete       delete the selected clip (Shift-Delete: leave a gap)
  F8  F9       undo, redo (100 steps)
  N            snapping on / off
  +  -         zoom the Timeline in, out
  Shift-Z  \   the whole project in the Timeline
  Ctrl-Z       clear the selection
  F3           save
  Escape       stop

Saving: the standard Save as box: drag the file icon to a directory
display, or type a full name and press Return. Projects are MLT XML
files (filetype XML, &F80), which Shotcut and Kdenlive open too. Closing
the editor with changes not saved asks first.

While playing, the picture goes through the hardware overlay (VideoOverlay)
when it can; a menu or window over the picture, or stopping, draws it in
the window instead. *Set ReelEdit$NoOverlay 1 never uses the overlay.

Log
---
Reel Edit writes what it did to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.ReelEditLog (each time it
starts). Please send it with any report. *Set ReelEdit$Log to another
file, or "off".

